 ## Overview
-From-region visibility preprocessing is a technique that precomputes which parts of a 3D scene are visible from specific viewing regions to accelerate rendering. It solves the problem of expensive per-frame visibility determination for large datasets by shifting the computational load to an offline phase. Unlike sibling methods that rely on axis-aligned voxel occupancy checks [923-voxel-block-placement-with-face-culling.md] or simple backface culling [4569-voxel-backface-culling-and-internal-face-masking.md], this approach handles complex geometry and occluder fusion, though it incurs significant preprocessing overhead.
+From-region visibility preprocessing is a technique for optimizing the rendering of large-scale 3D environments by precomputing which geometric primitives are visible from specific regions of the scene. It solves the problem of expensive per-frame visibility culling for massive datasets by shifting the computational burden to an offline phase. It is distinguished from its siblings, such as [Face Culling and Visibility Optimization](3861-face-culling-and-visibility-optimization.md), which rely on local voxel topology and axis-aligned assumptions, by operating on general geometry (triangles) and using occluder fusion to reduce the number of visible primitives, albeit at the cost of significantly longer preprocessing times [S1, S2, S3, S4, S5].
 
 ## How it works
-The method involves precomputing visibility information for a set of predefined regions in the scene. The core algorithm, as presented by Wonka et al. [S1, unofficial], utilizes occluder fusion to reduce the number of primitives considered for visibility tests.
+The method involves offline preprocessing to generate visibility data structures that map viewing regions to subsets of the scene geometry.
 
